SHIPPED TOOLCHAINS (an installed deployment carries these beside the
binary, under `toolchains/`, so the deployment can rebuild itself):

- bun, at exactly the version the release was built with (recorded in
  `toolchains/toolchains.json`). Bun is developed by Oven and distributed
  under the MIT license; its license text ships with bun's own distribution
  at https://bun.sh and is not vendored here.
- The tree-sitter grammar wasm files (44 of them), which are BUILD OUTPUTS,
  not source: each one is the compiled form of a crate declared in
  `scripts/build-ast-packs.ts` (id, crate name and version are recorded per
  file in `toolchains/toolchains.json`). Each grammar is therefore covered
  by its own crate's license, and that license's text ships with the
  crate's source on crates.io rather than with this binary. Building them
  requires cargo and network access to crates.io, which is why the release
  ships them prebuilt rather than making a rebuild depend on both.

The npm dependency tree (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt) does not cover either of
these: they are not installed packages. The attribution above is the record
for them, and `scripts/build-standalone.ts` writes the manifest that names
every file the release actually carries.
